A||||5||MrPBody||Decapitating the Duke (a hatchet job)||||7||
Z||000000||MrPBody||03-24-2001||12:28 PM||||Further adventures with a stuck Duke head . . .<P>I didn't hang the car by its head after all.  To loosen the head, I drove a wedge between the bellhousing / block flange and the head overhang.  The wedge?  A hatchet head.  (Broken handle; I knew I was saving it for something.)<P>After a few more hours of applying hammer, crowbar, torch, WD-40, Gumout, and profanity, <B>the head is off!</B>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>My best guess at this point: the head gasket was venting combustion gas into the head bolt passage, surrounding the head bolt with <I>x</I> years of baked carbonaceous crud.  When I got the head off, I was able to unscrew the head bolt stub from the block with my fingers.  Evidently, the bolt broke entirely because it was stuck to the head!<P>Now, back to scraping the block . . .<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||03-24-2001||12:55 PM||||So do you need another head?<BR>  crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||03-24-2001||03:50 PM||||Fierowrecker --<P>I already have a rebuilt head, thanks.  And I don't think I mangled this one beyond use.  I wouldn't be surprised if it's cracked, though.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||03-24-2001||04:15 PM||||Off with his head!!<P>Way to go. ||||reg||14
Z||000004||Turbo1dr||03-24-2001||10:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MrPBody:<BR><B>Further adventures with a stuck Duke head . . .<P><BR>My best guess at this point: the head gasket was venting combustion gas into the head bolt passage, surrounding the head bolt with <I>x</I> years of baked carbonaceous crud.  When I got the head off, I was able to unscrew the head bolt stub from the block with my fingers.  Evidently, the bolt broke entirely because it was stuck to the head!<P>Now, back to scraping the block . . .</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, the Iron Duke engine was bad about the center head bolt (between the exhaust ports) to break off and cause the gasket to leak. <BR><P>------------------<BR>86 SE Fiero converted to GT (Buick Turbo V6 transplant in progress)<P><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.||||reg||1
Z||000005||MrPBody||03-25-2001||10:12 AM||||Turbo1dr --<P>As far as I can tell, the bolt was intact until I leaned on it with a 1/2"-drive flex handle and cheater pipe.  The head gasket looked pretty decrepit: split cylinder sealing rings and many discolored areas.<BR>||||reg||1
